# MAGIC <div class="dbx">
# MAGIC <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><span class="sec-icon">🧞</span><h2>Why this matters for Genie</h2></div><p>When Genie only sees raw tables, it has to <strong>guess</strong> your business logic each time someone asks a question:</p>
# MAGIC <ul>
# MAGIC <li><em>"What is revenue?"</em> Gross or net? Including cancelled orders? Including test orders?</li>
# MAGIC <li><em>"Average order value"</em> means <code>SUM(revenue) / COUNT(orders)</code>, not <code>AVG(price)</code>.</li>
# MAGIC <li><em>"Active customers"</em> is a <code>COUNT(DISTINCT …)</code>, which <strong>cannot be summed</strong> across months.</li>
# MAGIC </ul>
# MAGIC <p>Each guess is a chance for <strong>two users asking the same question to get two different numbers</strong>.</p>
# MAGIC <p>A metric view moves that logic out of the LLM and into a <strong>governed, versioned definition</strong>:</p>
# MAGIC <div class="tbl"><table>
# MAGIC <thead>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <th>Without metric view</th>
# MAGIC <th>With metric view</th>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC </thead>
# MAGIC <tbody>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td>Genie writes the aggregation SQL itself</td>
# MAGIC <td>Genie picks a measure: <code>MEASURE(net_revenue)</code></td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td>Business rules live in instructions / examples</td>
# MAGIC <td>Business rules live in Unity Catalog (one place)</td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td>Same KPI can differ between Genie, dashboards and Excel</td>
# MAGIC <td>Same definition everywhere</td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC <tr>
# MAGIC <td>Hard to review</td>
# MAGIC <td>Reviewable YAML, permissions, lineage</td>
# MAGIC </tr>
# MAGIC </tbody>
# MAGIC </table></div>
# MAGIC </div>

