# MAGIC <div class="dbx">
# MAGIC <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><span class="sec-icon">📏</span><h2>What is a metric view?</h2></div><p>A <strong>metric view</strong> is a Unity Catalog object, defined in YAML, that separates:</p>
# MAGIC <ul>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Source:</strong> a table, view, SQL query, or another metric view (optionally with <strong>joins</strong> in a star or snowflake schema)</li>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Filter:</strong> a condition applied to <em>every</em> query (e.g. <code>status &lt;&gt; 'TEST'</code>)</li>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Fields (dimensions):</strong> the attributes you group and filter by (<code>region</code>, <code>order_month</code>, …)</li>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Measures:</strong> aggregate expressions (<code>SUM(...)</code>, <code>COUNT(DISTINCT ...)</code>, ratios, window measures)</li>
# MAGIC </ul>
# MAGIC <p>The key difference from a normal view:</p>
# MAGIC <ul>
# MAGIC <li>A <strong>standard view</strong> fixes the aggregation <em>and</em> the grouping when you create it.</li>
# MAGIC <li>A <strong>metric view</strong> fixes only the <strong>definition</strong> of the measure. The <strong>grouping is chosen at query time</strong>, and the engine generates the correct SQL for that grain.</li>
# MAGIC </ul>
# MAGIC <blockquote class="callout">
# MAGIC <p>Metric views are the core of the <strong>Unity Catalog semantics layer</strong> (docs: <em>UC Semantics → Metric views</em>).</p>
# MAGIC </blockquote>
# MAGIC </div>

