# MAGIC <div class="dbx">
# MAGIC <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">10</span><span class="sec-icon">📊</span><h2>What's new: metric views in AI/BI Dashboards</h2></div><p>There are three ways to use metric views in a dashboard (<strong>Data</strong> tab → <strong>Add data</strong>):</p>
# MAGIC <ol>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Unity Catalog metric view as a dataset.</strong> You get every dimension and measure. You <strong>can't</strong> change the dataset query (no filters or column pruning), but you <strong>can</strong> add custom calculations.</li>
# MAGIC <li><strong>SQL dataset that references a metric view.</strong> Use this when you need filters or reshaping. Measures go through <code>MEASURE()</code>. Parameters can be passed, e.g. <code>sales_metrics(discount =&gt; :discount)</code> linked to a filter widget.</li>
# MAGIC <li><strong>Local metric view (dashboard metric view).</strong> Define dimensions, measures and joins <strong>scoped to the dashboard</strong>, without publishing anything to Unity Catalog. Picking a table or view as the source opens the local metric view editor.</li>
# MAGIC </ol>
# MAGIC <p><strong>Export as a metric view (Public Preview):</strong> dataset kebab menu → <em>Create Metric View</em> → pick catalog and schema → optionally edit the YAML → <strong>Save</strong>. Measures and dimensions defined on the dataset are carried over. This <strong>promotes</strong> a dashboard-only definition into a governed UC metric view that Genie can use as well.</p>
# MAGIC </div>

