# MAGIC <div class="dbx">
# MAGIC <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">12</span><span class="sec-icon">🧞</span><h2>Metric views + Genie Agents</h2></div><blockquote class="callout">
# MAGIC <p><strong>Naming:</strong> <em>Genie Agents were formerly known as Genie Spaces.</em> The course may still say "Genie space".</p>
# MAGIC </blockquote>
# MAGIC <ul>
# MAGIC <li>Add a metric view to a Genie Agent exactly like a table: <strong>up to 50 tables, views or metric views</strong> per agent.</li>
# MAGIC <li>The Genie Agent needs a <strong>pro or serverless SQL warehouse</strong>.</li>
# MAGIC <li>Genie generates <code>MEASURE(...)</code> queries, and <strong>synonyms are imported automatically</strong>.</li>
# MAGIC <li>The reverse direction exists too: a <strong>Genie Agent's context can be exported as a metric view</strong> (kebab menu → <em>Export to metric view</em>). This turns tuned instructions and SQL expressions into a governed definition.</li>
# MAGIC </ul>
# MAGIC <p><strong>Recommended pattern for reliable agents:</strong></p>
# MAGIC <ol>
# MAGIC <li>Model the KPIs in a metric view (comments, synonyms, formats).</li>
# MAGIC <li>Give the Genie Agent the <strong>metric view</strong> (and only the raw tables it really needs).</li>
# MAGIC <li>Keep instructions short, focused on <em>business context</em>, not on SQL formulas.</li>
# MAGIC <li>Add <strong>benchmarks</strong> with questions whose expected SQL uses <code>MEASURE()</code>.</li>
# MAGIC </ol>
# MAGIC </div>

